Ladybird skips a source element whose non-empty type attribute
canPlayType() answers with the empty string, before setting currentSrc
or fetching it. Blink’s HTMLMediaElement::SelectNextSourceChild(),
WebKit’s HTMLMediaElement::selectNextSourceChild() and Gecko’s
HTMLMediaElement::LoadFromSourceChildren() each skip such a source too,
and also treat an empty type attribute as no type at all.

Chrome 157.0.8103.78 and Firefox 151.0 agree with Ladybird now on eight
cases: a source with an unsupported type, a supported type, an empty
type, an unparseable type, application/octet-stream, an unsupported
codec, an HLS source followed by an MP4 one, and an unsupported source
followed by one with no type. (Playwright’s WebKit 26.5 build plays no
media, so it selects no source in any of them.)

Blink and WebKit also take the type of a source with no type attribute
from its src, when that’s a data: URL, and skip the source if they
can’t play that type. Gecko doesn’t, and neither does the spec, so
Ladybird doesn’t either: Firefox 151.0 and Ladybird select a data:
URL with an unplayable type, and Chrome 157.0.8103.78 skips it.
